What: waitForEndpoint gets a probe kind per endpoint (none = TCP connect, status = toolkit GET /mcp/status as k205, ping = new) and returns whether the watch ended on an answer. New unrealMCPEndpoint (probePing): pingProbe POSTs {"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":1,"method":"ping"} to unreal.mcp's URL with no session (Content-Type application/json, Accept application/json + text/event-stream); up = HTTP 200 whose body (or an event-stream data line) is a JSON-RPC 2.0 response with id 1, a result object and no error. An open port, 405, 404, 3xx, 5xx, an error object, a null result or HTML are all not-ready, kept as the warning's last answer. agentHeldForUnrealMCP(cfg) = unreal.mcp.enabled && agent.enabled && !unreal.manualStart. Held: the agent service gets Autostart false; after sup.Start run() logs (and alerts in window mode) one INFO line and runs holdAgentForUnrealMCP, which waits endpointWaitWindow (5 min) and then calls sup.StartService(agent); at the window's end exactly one WARN (prepLogger: log + alert) with the hint (or editorNotRunning's sentence) plus 'the agent starts now without it: ...'; ctx done (signal, q) -> no start, no line. Watch gate in run(): cfg.MCP.Enabled && (len(ExtraServers) > 0 || cfg.Unreal.MCP.Enabled). prepareMCPBridges watches unreal-mcp by ping when not held (manualStart, no agent), not when held (the hold is the watch, so one line). Hand-written http extraServers keep the bare TCP watch, no hold.
